![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to t](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)

Multiply along each path and add the contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}t}&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}t}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}t}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\cos{(x )} \cos{(y )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- \sin{(x )} \sin{(y )}
\end{aligned}
$$

For the negative arguments, use $\sin(-u)=-\sin(u)$ and $\cos(-u)=\cos(u)$.

Substitute both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\cos{((- t^{2}) )} \cos{((- t) )} \\ &=\cos{(t )} \cos{(t^{2} )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- \sin{((- t^{2}) )} \sin{((- t) )} \\ &=- \sin{(t )} \sin{(t^{2} )}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}t}&=- 2 t \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}t}&=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute each path product, then add.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}t}&=(\cos{(t )} \cos{(t^{2} )})(- 2 t) \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}t}&=(- \sin{(t )} \sin{(t^{2} )})(-1) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}t}&=\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \cos{(t )} \cos{(t^{2} )}\\&{}+(\sin{(t )} \sin{(t^{2} )})\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
$$

![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)

Multiply along each path and add the contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\frac{1}{y + 1} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- \frac{x}{(y + 1)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\frac{1}{s + 1} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- \frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=2 s \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute each path product, then add.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\left(\frac{1}{s + 1}\right)(2 s) \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\left(- \frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}\right)(1) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\begin{aligned}&\frac{2 s}{s + 1}\\&{}+\left(- \frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}\right)\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
$$

![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)

Multiply along each path and add the contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\cos{(y )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- x \sin{(y )}
\end{aligned}
$$

For the negative arguments, use $\sin(-u)=-\sin(u)$ and $\cos(-u)=\cos(u)$.

Substitute both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\cos{((- s) )} \\ &=\cos{(s )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=- (- 3 s) \sin{((- s) )} \\ &=- 3 s \sin{(s )}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=-3 \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute each path product, then add.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=(\cos{(s )})(-3) \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=(- 3 s \sin{(s )})(-1) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\begin{aligned}&- 3 \cos{(s )}\\&{}+(3 s \sin{(s )})\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
$$

![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)

Multiply along each path and add the contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\ln{(y )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=\frac{x}{y} + 1
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\ln{(2 s )} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=2 \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute each path product, then add.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=(\ln{(2 s )})(2) \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=(2)(2) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\begin{aligned}&2 \ln{(2 s )}\\&{}+(4)\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
$$

![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)

Multiply along each path and add the contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\frac{y}{\sqrt{2xy}} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=\frac{x}{\sqrt{2xy}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute both coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=\frac{\ln{(2 s )}}{\sqrt{2(2 s)(\ln{(2 s )})}} \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}&=\frac{2 s}{\sqrt{2(2 s)(\ln{(2 s )})}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate the coordinate functions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=2 \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{1}{s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Compute each path product, then add.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\mathrm{d}x}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\left(\frac{\ln{(2 s )}}{\sqrt{2(2 s)(\ln{(2 s )})}}\right)(2) \\ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\mathrm{d}y}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\left(\frac{2 s}{\sqrt{2(2 s)(\ln{(2 s )})}}\right)\left(\frac{1}{s}\right) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d}f}{\mathrm{d}s}&=\frac{2 \ln{(2 s )} + (2)}{\sqrt{2(2 s)(\ln{(2 s )})}}
\end{aligned}
$$

