First, compute the ordered cross product $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf{i}&\mathbf{j}&\mathbf{k}\\1&0&2\\2&-3&-2\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Its components are

$$
\begin{aligned}
d_1&=0\cdot(-2)-2\cdot(-3)=6 \\
d_2&=-(1\cdot(-2)-2\cdot2)=6 \\
d_3&=1\cdot(-3)-0\cdot2=-3
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}=\langle6,6,-3\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\langle-1,3,-2\rangle\cdot\langle6,6,-3\rangle \\
&=(-1)\cdot6+3\cdot6+(-2)\cdot(-3) \\
&=(-6)+18+6 \\
&=18
\end{aligned}
$$

First, compute the ordered cross product $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf{i}&\mathbf{j}&\mathbf{k}\\-2&-2&-2\\0&3&0\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Its components are

$$
\begin{aligned}
d_1&=(-2)\cdot0-(-2)\cdot3=6 \\
d_2&=-((-2)\cdot0-(-2)\cdot0)=0 \\
d_3&=(-2)\cdot3-(-2)\cdot0=-6
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}=\langle6,0,-6\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\langle2,1,-3\rangle\cdot\langle6,0,-6\rangle \\
&=2\cdot6+1\cdot0+(-3)\cdot(-6) \\
&=12+0+18 \\
&=30
\end{aligned}
$$

First, compute the ordered cross product $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}&=\begin{vmatrix}\mathbf{i}&\mathbf{j}&\mathbf{k}\\3&-1&3\\0&-3&-2\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Its components are

$$
\begin{aligned}
d_1&=(-1)\cdot(-2)-3\cdot(-3)=11 \\
d_2&=-(3\cdot(-2)-3\cdot0)=6 \\
d_3&=3\cdot(-3)-(-1)\cdot0=-9
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}=\langle11,6,-9\rangle$. Now take the dot product with $\mathbf{a}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\langle3,3,2\rangle\cdot\langle11,6,-9\rangle \\
&=3\cdot11+3\cdot6+2\cdot(-9) \\
&=33+18+(-18) \\
&=33
\end{aligned}
$$

